WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:05.628
Filmmakers use a lot of tricks 
to up the anxiety of a scene.

00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:07.160
Music...

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.066
Sound mixing...

00:00:09.066 --> 00:00:11.015
Lighting, color...

00:00:11.015 --> 00:00:13.589
And then there’s this trick:

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:17.665
An ever-so-slight tilt of the camera.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.857
Even without all of the other stuff,

00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:23.163
the second you take the world 
off its axis,

00:00:23.163 --> 00:00:25.677
things start to feel a little off.

00:00:25.677 --> 00:00:28.888
Or at least a little more interesting.

00:00:28.888 --> 00:00:31.793
That camera tilt,
which you'll see everywhere

00:00:31.793 --> 00:00:34.493
from blockbuster movies
to perfume commercials,

00:00:34.493 --> 00:00:36.141
it’s called a lot of things.

00:00:36.141 --> 00:00:39.615
But most often, you’ll see it referred to
as the “dutch angle.”

00:00:39.615 --> 00:00:44.705
It’s a stylistic choice whose roots 
are based not in film, but in fine art.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.972
Let’s get this out of the way.

00:00:51.972 --> 00:00:54.440
The Dutch angle isn’t actually Dutch.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:55.739
It’s Deutsch.

00:00:55.739 --> 00:00:58.109
As in German for German.

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:02.052
Its origins trace back to World War I, 
when the German government banned 

00:01:02.052 --> 00:01:04.178
the import of foreign films.

00:01:05.649 --> 00:01:09.050
So while, over in Hollywood,
the movie business was booming

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.470
with Charlie Chaplin joints,

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.460
racist Tarzan adaptations,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.220
and other straightforward, 
happy-ending-type flicks,

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.193
in Germany, movies were starting
to look a little different.

00:01:20.193 --> 00:01:24.990
Instead of turning to Hollywood for inspiration,
German filmmakers turned to the arts.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.729
And at the time, a movement called 
“Expressionism” had taken hold,

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:31.235
primarily in Germany and Austria.

00:01:31.235 --> 00:01:34.224
Expressionism is famously hard to define.

00:01:34.224 --> 00:01:38.544
But you can think of it as roughly 
the opposite of impressionism.

00:01:38.544 --> 00:01:43.336
Impressionists like Monet, Degas,
and Renoir painted things like this.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:46.147
Floaty, lovely, soft scenes.

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:51.908
And expressionists like Kirchner, Beckmann,
and Dix painted things like this.

00:01:51.908 --> 00:01:55.042
One more time, for emphasis.

00:01:56.518 --> 00:01:58.939
While Impressionists were
interested in producing

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:02.532
spontaneous, but always
refined versions of reality,

00:02:02.532 --> 00:02:08.419
Expressionist painters were focused on depicting
a subjective version of a world in turmoil,

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:12.360
grappling with the horrors of war 
and the anxiety of modern life.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.980
Figures are distorted and painted roughly,
often tossed about at impossible angles.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.496
Colors are intense and unrealistic.

00:02:20.496 --> 00:02:23.631
Scenes are eerie, even nightmarish.

00:02:23.631 --> 00:02:27.872
It’s harsh and dark, in both 
content and execution.

00:02:27.872 --> 00:02:30.901
All of which translated pretty well to film.

00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:35.240
Starting in 1920, with
The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari:

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.087
A German silent horror movie
about a hypnotist

00:02:38.087 --> 00:02:42.000
who uses his sleepwalking
patients to kill by proxy.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.535
It’s got the distorted figures,
by way of outlandish makeup,

00:02:45.535 --> 00:02:47.151
harsh angular lighting,

00:02:47.151 --> 00:02:50.717
and, in place of the exaggerated 
perspective of painting,

00:02:50.717 --> 00:02:54.080
the whole movie is noticeably askew.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.030
But not because of camera work.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.990
The set is literally tilted, with 
sidewalks that lead nowhere,

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:02.390
walls arranged seemingly at random,

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.967
and buildings that look
poised to collapse;

00:03:04.967 --> 00:03:11.647
all rendered in stark, graphic lines,
by a trio of expressionist painters.

00:03:11.647 --> 00:03:15.896
When The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari
came out, it was a revelation.

00:03:15.896 --> 00:03:17.303
Critics called it startling!

00:03:17.303 --> 00:03:17.803
Weird!

00:03:17.803 --> 00:03:18.880
Sensational!

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.046
"The most remarkable picture ever shown."

00:03:22.046 --> 00:03:24.685
They lauded its great acting 
and innovative story.

00:03:24.685 --> 00:03:27.230
There’s a killer surprise twist at the end.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:31.026
But, mostly, it was that set design 
that excited people.

00:03:31.026 --> 00:03:35.825
Those turns and tilts enhanced
the movie’s suspense and distortion.

00:03:35.825 --> 00:03:41.409
Caligari was hailed a "prophecy," ushering
in a new art-centric era for movies.

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:45.759
And, though critics couldn’t quite yet agree
on which art movement it belonged to,

00:03:45.759 --> 00:03:48.333
that prophecy kind of came true.

00:03:48.333 --> 00:03:52.303
Expressionist classics like Metropolis
and Nosferatu soon followed,

00:03:52.303 --> 00:03:56.501
using that same lighting, drama,
and of course, dutch angles --

00:03:56.501 --> 00:04:00.853
this time, using the camera to
tilt the scene, instead of the set.

00:04:02.058 --> 00:04:04.533
From there, the dutch angle
spread to noir films,

00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:08.343
featuring prominently in
Citizen Kane and the Third Man.

00:04:08.343 --> 00:04:13.167
Then, it broke into thrillers, specifically
Hitchcock thrillers, like The Birds,

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:16.105
Strangers on a Train, and
the lesser-known, The Lodger.

00:04:16.105 --> 00:04:20.269
Which the man himself said was
heavily influenced by expressionism.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:23.620
"That picture shared a
strong German influence."

00:04:24.496 --> 00:04:28.127
By the 60s, a very different genre 
picked it up.

00:04:28.127 --> 00:04:33.608
1966’s Batman famously gave each villain
their own specific tilt.

00:04:33.608 --> 00:04:36.318
And from there the angle really took off.

00:04:36.318 --> 00:04:37.248
Action movies.

00:04:37.248 --> 00:04:37.919
Musicals.

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:38.919
Comedies.

00:04:38.919 --> 00:04:40.349
Even commercials.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:48.706
Today, it's featured across basically every
genre, to varying degrees of success.

00:04:48.706 --> 00:04:52.432
Movies like 2000’s Battlefield Earth
and 2011's Thor

00:04:52.432 --> 00:04:56.464
have been criticized for
using the tilt as a crutch.

00:04:56.464 --> 00:05:02.167
But filmmakers like Spike Lee, Terry Gilliam,
and Tim Burton all seem to get it:

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:04.130
Tilting a shot to highlight tension,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.424
or distortion,

00:05:05.424 --> 00:05:08.850
or underscore a film’s
dystopian confusion.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.925
Keeping the spirit of
expressionism alive.

